{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for deployment round 15 at exact clean head 6860a4301626e2ed07617dba51003788fdffb66d, against request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cec220f47e6366795dfca0b7fcbeae684677177e, promise git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2c81139c81041171de0b999fb45946f4aa54aa23, and artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6188de3a5bff25a73df7c35c708d677db6ba688d.\n\n1. The computed root does not prevent a hidden-history reset, and the revised hostile case asserts a false topology. Let L be the current ledger head and R the genuine computed root. A drain can append F with first parent R and L as a non-first parent. Updating the ledger ref L→F is a valid fast-forward because L is a parent of F. A first-parent walk is F→R and terminates successfully at the exact known root while omitting every batch from L backwards. The note says this case is rejected because “the walk never terminates at the computed root”; it does. Role checks on side parents might reject L for another reason if they were fully specified, but that is not the termination property the case claims to test. Bind each entry to the actual previous ledger tip with an independently checkable rule, or retain a bounded way to reject any old-ledger side parent, and make the hostile case construct R as first parent so it can fail for the intended reason.\n\n2. Parent roles are still asserted rather than mechanically discriminated. The correction supplies the missing profile position, but predecessor, profile, E and candidates are all Git commits. “Type and content” does not define a profile schema, trusted signature/lineage, or a ledger-entry authentication rule, and attacker-authored commits can imitate untrusted content. This matters directly to finding 1: the claimed safety now relies on proving the side-parent L cannot occupy any non-predecessor role. Specify the exact grammar, signatures, ancestry and trust anchors that distinguish every position, then test a commit-shaped impostor in each role.\n\n3. The profile bundle still retains rules bytes without retaining executable closure. The round-14 text is unchanged: a declarative ruleset plus an interpreter-contract version is called self-contained and the note says an auditor holding that commit can execute it. A version string is an external dependency and does not supply an interpreter or its dependencies. The independent GC probe already established byte retention, not executability. Retain a content-addressed executable/dependency closure or state and enforce the external compatibility/availability obligation; add a control that fails when only rules plus a version string survive.\n\n4. The first-batch base chain needs an explicit sentinel rule. The computed root's canonical message contains only “gitseq-ledger-root \u003cgenesis-hex\u003e”; it has no head field. Later text tells the auditor to read predecessor.head and says the root's head is the genesis commit. That can be a valid virtual value derived from the root message, but it is not encoded or specified as the parser's root special case. Define that derivation explicitly and test the first real batch against the actual genesis object.\n\nIndependent root probe: fresh SHA-1 and SHA-256 bare repositories can create their empty trees; with fixed gitseq identity, 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z dates and the stated message, repeated commit-tree runs produced identical roots within each object format (SHA-1 28ed9b9f6570a694e79b3a3d39265010dacdfd06; SHA-256 366791a7b12de05b466f3d8f03a258e7b065228ce625afa5d5c77979eec6363d). This closes reproducibility on the installed Git version. The note should still name exact serialized bytes or the required commit-tree/mktree procedure, including the trailing message newline, for cross-version interoperability.\n\nThe ref-creator repair is closed: claimed namespace/ref bytes are now explicitly untrusted and unattributed. The valid-alias contradiction is closed: one canonical ref produces the ordinary line and each extra ref produces a separate noncanonical-ref record with length-prefixed bytes. The entries and old refs-list grammar do not survive except in clearly historical explanations. No merge performed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6188de3a5bff25a73df7c35c708d677db6ba688d","head":"6860a4301626e2ed07617dba51003788fdffb66d","probes":"whole-note consistency sweep; SHA-1/SHA-256 root construction; empty-tree creation; forged first-parent topology; profile/parent role audit","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cec220f47e6366795dfca0b7fcbeae684677177e","verdict":"changes-requested"}}